	<key>readme</key>
	<string>## Setup

To copy directly from Apple Passwords, install apwcli once:

`brew install michel-tricot/tap/apwcli`

Then run `pwsetup` in Alfred and complete the local Apple PIN pairing.
Requires a supported Chromium browser. apwcli downloads Apple's iCloud Passwords extension and runs it in a dedicated background browser profile.
It is a third-party integration, not an Apple-supported CLI.

Requires macOS Sequoia or later. No Accessibility permission is needed.

## Usage

* `pass github.com [username]`: copy a password from Apple Passwords.
* `pcode github.com [username]`: copy its current verification code.
* If several accounts match, specify the username; nothing is copied until the match is unique. Enter usernames literally, without shell quotes.

The new commands do not expose credentials to Alfred's debugger or notifications. Clipboard privacy markers request exclusion from history.
Run `apwcli doctor` to diagnose the connection and `apwcli daemon stop` to stop the helper. Re-run `pwsetup` if pairing expires.

The existing standalone OTP generator needs no apwcli setup:

Copy a time-based verification code for a stored account via the `otp` keyword.

* &lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Copy the current code.
* &lt;kbd&gt;⌥&lt;/kbd&gt;&lt;kbd&gt;↩&lt;/kbd&gt; Forget this account.

Add an account by copying its secret or `otpauth://` link, then using the `otpadd` keyword. Give it a name, or leave it blank to take the name from the link. Your clipboard is cleared once the seed is stored.

The clipboard is cleared after the delay set in the Workflow’s Configuration, but only if it still holds the copied value.

The `otp` command generates codes locally from the stored seed (RFC 6238), independently of Apple Passwords. Use `pcode` for codes in Apple Passwords.
</string>
